WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.969
Film Courage: In the Kickstarter video [for
CURRENT SEA] you have an image of a man fighting

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:07.241
off another man on the boat or there’s a
fight that takes place, so we see that [where

00:00:07.241 --> 00:00:08.680
the movie is shot] it’s a turbulent environment.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.369
With some of the filming that you’ve been
exposed to, are you worried about your own

00:00:13.369 --> 00:00:14.369
personal safety?

00:00:14.369 --> 00:00:18.700
Stephanie Lincoln: I’m worried about Christopher’s
safety all the time when he’s there and

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.740
I’m not there.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.770
Christopher Smith: Really?

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.500
You never told me that.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.680
Well my mom is, I know that much.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.330
And now Stephanie.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:32.410
You know, filming in Cambodia in particular
(I’ll just speak for that) because it’s

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:38.000
probably different in different countries
but you kind of get a pass a bit for being

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.130
a Westerner.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:44.310
And you have a certain degree of protection
that locals don’t have.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:49.010
And so a lot of journalists who are Cambodian,
a lot of environmentalists that are Cambodian

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:54.900
are either imprisoned or sometimes disappear
or there are some prominent cases of some

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.960
very public bad things happening that of course
never really get solved or tied to anybody.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.660
It is a lot more dangerous for them than it
is for people like us going in.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:11.100
However, when you are out on the open oceans,
a lot of things can accidentally happen and

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.310
actually we (in the course of making this
film) learned about a local Cambodian champ

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.810
fisherman who was hurt by a Vietnamese fisherman
and of course no one was held responsible

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.520
for that.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.960
So there is a certain amount of danger for
sure and particularly on these boats, they

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.770
are really small and there’s not a lot of
places to run and hide, you can’t just run

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:39.770
away.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:40.770
It’s like you’re stuck there.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:46.670
There was a few moments which isn’t in the
Kickstarter video (it might not even be in

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.940
the film) but where we were out on patrol
and that’s what MCC calls it when they go

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.689
out an look for illegal fisherman, it’s
called Patrol.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:58.260
Not to necessarily go looking but when they
kind of come in their area they’ll kind

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.030
of go out to try to stop them and they take
the nets.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.110
And so when we do some of those things they
are usually at night because that is when

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:10.009
illegal fisherman are operating and they operate
without lights so you go out there and it’s

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.060
pitch black.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.580
And Stephanie, when we went out there the
first time we had an encounter.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:20.161
So then what happens is they’ll like kind
of creep up on the illegal fisherman and then

00:02:20.161 --> 00:02:22.319
they will turn on their lights and start shining
lights on them.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:26.060
But then the fisherman have brighter lights
that they shine back on you and they usually

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.080
fish in groups and so what they do is they
drop their nets and they start circling you

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.489
and there is lots of yelling in Khmer which
is the language of Cambodia.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:41.130
And it’s actually really kind of scary!

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.950
It might not come off that way in the video,
but in the moment it’s like anything can

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.170
happen.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:51.099
One time I was there (the second time) we
went out and we didn’t have the advantage…it

00:02:51.099 --> 00:02:57.040
was a new moon so there was no light basically
and you could barely make out some shapes

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.590
and all of the sudden we pretty much realized
that we were surrounded four or five giant…actually

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:07.730
I think it might have been as much as seven
Vietnamese paratrawlers and you can hear on

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.050
the radio and their lights start to come in
when they see you…everybody knows who Paul

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.569
is [one of the subject’s in CURRENT SEA]
and what they do.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:18.019
So they are on the lookout for Paul and that’s
why they travel in groups so then they can

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:21.800
kind of combat them better.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.910
At that point they kind of surrounded us and
we actually moved away because we were worried

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:32.739
about getting too far away from…they have
a medium sized boat, a speed boat and a big

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:33.739
boat.

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:41.040
And if we were too far away from reinforcements
in the big boat then it could be an issue.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.720
Nothing ended up happening that night, but
there are moments where…when it feels in

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.440
control, it’s not as scary.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.239
But the moment where you feel that this has
now gotten out of control, that’s when your

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:56.769
warning bells go off.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:04:02.500
Having said that, nothing has happened so
far.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.670
I think that legally speaking like getting
in trouble with the people in charge of the

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:11.569
country there is some risk there in terms
of they could decide that they just don’t

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:14.970
want to tolerate us anymore and I don’t
think they would necessarily get us in trouble

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.139
but they could definitely kick us out of the
country and say we can’t come back again

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:21.969
and then we would be able to finish our movie.

00:04:21.969 --> 00:04:30.360
There is a little bit of that fear, too, which
I think is a little unfair in a way.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:40.249
Not unfair…but we are trying to do with
our movie what is best for Cambodia which

00:04:40.249 --> 00:04:44.589
is shed light on this sort of issue because
a lot of these illegal fisherman are really

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:49.219
what they are doing besides destroying the
environment which as Westerners is why were

00:04:49.219 --> 00:04:50.479
are probably initially drawn to it.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:55.850
But in terms of the people in the country,
there’s a lot of people who live off of

00:04:55.850 --> 00:05:01.419
the ocean and subsistence fishing who are
having a harder and harder time being able

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:05.849
to do that because the fish are disappearing,
the habitat for the fish is disappearing,

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:11.749
they’ll put their crab posts in or their
own smaller nets that just get taken up by

00:05:11.749 --> 00:05:17.180
the big fishing boats and they lose their
livelihood and they also get into skirmishes

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:25.770
with these larger illegal fisherman and so
there is people there who kind of need this

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:30.270
to happen for them (not so much for the movie)
but for the work that Paul and Matt are doing

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:34.059
and so getting that out there to the larger
audience is going to be very helpful.

00:05:34.059 --> 00:05:38.129
And I would hope that the people in charge
of the country would understand that’s what

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:42.789
we are trying to do and we’re not there
to make them look bad but to try to uh…like…correct

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:44.199
some injustices, perhaps?

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:46.539
Stephanie Lincoln: Yeah and….[doorbell rings].

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:49.349
Christopher Smith: The mail is here!

